\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Testing is worth practicing. Your strategy doc lists "What would you test?" as part of the architecture discussion, and the 
\f1\fs26 InterviewPrepTests
\f0\fs24  target Xcode created is already waiting for tests. Here are the tests by layer, starting with the cheapest to write.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 1. Model decoding (easiest, start here)
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The full 
\f1\fs26 restaurants.json
\f0\fs24  decodes, with 8 restaurants and the right 
\f1\fs26 page
\f0\fs24  and 
\f1\fs26 totalPages
\f0\fs24 .\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A restaurant with 
\f1\fs26 "rating": null
\f0\fs24  decodes with 
\f1\fs26 rating == nil
\f0\fs24 .\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A restaurant with 
\f1\fs26 "image_url": null
\f0\fs24  or a missing 
\f1\fs26 promo
\f0\fs24  decodes without throwing.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A JSON snippet missing a required key, like 
\f1\fs26 name
\f0\fs24 , throws 
\f1\fs26 DecodingError.keyNotFound
\f0\fs24 . You can check this with 
\f1\fs26 #expect(throws:)
\f0\fs24 .\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Snake_case mapping works: 
\f1\fs26 zip_code
\f0\fs24  ends up in 
\f1\fs26 address.zipCode
\f0\fs24  and 
\f1\fs26 image_url
\f0\fs24  in 
\f1\fs26 imageUrl
\f0\fs24 . This would have caught the 
\f1\fs26 imageURL
\f0\fs24  naming trap from earlier.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 2. Pure logic
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your recall exercises become tests: the rating string for a nil and a non-nil rating, the open-only filter, the sort order with nil ratings last, 
\f1\fs26 PriceTier(level:)
\f0\fs24  returning 
\f1\fs26 nil
\f0\fs24  for out-of-range values, and the grouping by cuisine.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 3. View model (the most valuable one for the interview)
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The initial state is 
\f1\fs26 .idle
\f0\fs24 .\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 After 
\f1\fs26 loadData()
\f0\fs24  succeeds, the state is 
\f1\fs26 .loaded
\f0\fs24  with the expected restaurants.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 When loading fails, the state is 
\f1\fs26 .error
\f0\fs24  with a message.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The state passes through 
\f1\fs26 .loading
\f0\fs24  before it finishes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 For those you need to make the view model testable. Right now 
\f1\fs26 loadRestaurants()
\f0\fs24  reads 
\f1\fs26 Bundle.main
\f0\fs24  directly, so you can't make it fail on demand. Put the loading behind a protocol, for example 
\f1\fs26 protocol RestaurantService \{ func fetchRestaurants() async throws -> [Restaurant] \}
\f0\fs24 . The view model takes one in its initializer. In tests you pass a mock that returns fixed data or throws. This is what to say when asked "how would you structure this so it's testable?", and it's the point of MVVM.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 4. Networking (later, once you use 
\f3\fs26 URLSession
\f2\fs24 )
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Mock 
\f1\fs26 URLSession
\f0\fs24  with a 
\f1\fs26 URLProtocol
\f0\fs24  subclass to return canned responses.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cover a 200 response with valid JSON, a 404 or 500 status, invalid JSON, and no network.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 5. UI tests (low priority)
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The 
\f1\fs26 InterviewPrepUITests
\f0\fs24  target can launch the app and check that the list appears and a row opens the detail screen. They're slow and fragile, so for prep, know that they exist and why you'd write fewer of them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Xcode now supports two frameworks. Swift Testing (
\f1\fs26 import Testing
\f0\fs24 , 
\f1\fs26 @Test
\f0\fs24 , 
\f1\fs26 #expect
\f0\fs24 ) is the newer one, and XCTest (
\f1\fs26 XCTestCase
\f0\fs24 , 
\f1\fs26 XCTAssertEqual
\f0\fs24 ) is the older one. Many existing codebases, including probably DoorDash's, still use XCTest, so write a few tests in both styles. In a practice drill, one or two tests per layer is plenty.\
A good first exercise is the model decoding tests in item 1. Write them in the existing test target without help, and send them to me when you're done.\
